WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.025
For four lessons we’ve been reading lights.

2
00:00:03.275 --> 00:00:05.275
Today we use our ears.

3
00:00:05.525 --> 00:00:12.850
Sound signals are how two captains talk when they’re too far apart to shout, and the exam asks about them constantly.

4
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.550
Rule thirty-two gives us the vocabulary.

5
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.975
A whistle is any device that can make the required blasts.

6
00:00:21.225 --> 00:00:22.950
Your horn counts.

7
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.075
A short blast lasts about one second.

8
00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:29.475
A prolonged blast lasts four to six seconds.

9
00:00:29.725 --> 00:00:31.550
Short, one second.

10
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.825
Prolonged, four to six.

11
00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:38.550
Rule thirty-three then says what you must carry, and it goes by length.

12
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:46.500
Under twelve meters, you needn’t carry a whistle or a bell, but you must have some means of making an efficient sound signal.

13
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.550
That’s most of us.

14
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.650
A horn, or even a canister air horn, does the job.

15
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.675
Twelve meters and over: a whistle.

16
00:00:55.925 --> 00:00:59.300
Twenty meters and over: a whistle and a bell.

17
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:06.850
One hundred meters and over: a whistle, a bell, and a gong, with a tone that can’t be confused with the bell.

18
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:12.725
So the pattern is twelve, twenty, one hundred: whistle, then bell, then gong.
